WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.440
Today we are going to give instructions&nbsp;
to Google’s new self-driving robot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:18.660
and see if it is smart enough to deal with these&nbsp;
nasty tasks. Look at that. There is no way, right?

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:25.740
Well, with our current tools, I am not so&nbsp;
sure. But let’s be ambitious, and think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:33.240
a possible Dream Scenario for a self-driving&nbsp;
robot. For this, we need 3 puzzle pieces.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.160
You see, whenever we talk about self-driving&nbsp;
techniques, usually, the input is an image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.780
and the output is a question&nbsp;
like, okay self-driving car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:51.000
what would you do next? But note that the input&nbsp;
is not a sentence. These AIs don’t speak English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:58.800
so puzzle piece number one. Language. For a&nbsp;
possible solution to that, have a look at OpenAI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:06.300
and NVIDIA’s AI’s that can play Minecraft, and&nbsp;
here comes the best part: they also understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:13.860
English. How do we do that? Well, we can&nbsp;
use OpenAI’s earlier technique called GPT-3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:20.580
which has read a huge part of the internet,&nbsp;
and get this, it now understands English.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:29.340
However, we are not done yet. Not even close.&nbsp;
Puzzle piece number two. Images! If we wish it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:36.180
not only to be able to understand English, but&nbsp;
to understand our instructions, it needs to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:44.640
able to connect these English words to images. It&nbsp;
knows how to spell “stop sign”, but it hasn’t the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.820
slightest clue what a stop sign really looks&nbsp;
like. So, we need to add something that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:57.900
make it understand what it sees. Fortunately,&nbsp;
we can also do this, for instance, using the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:04.020
recent DALL-E AI that took the world by storm by&nbsp;
being able to generate these incredible images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:12.300
given our text descriptions. This has a back and&nbsp;
forth understanding between words and images, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:19.680
module is called CLIP, and it is a general puzzle&nbsp;
piece that can fortunately be reused here too.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:28.620
But, we are not done yet. Now, puzzle piece number&nbsp;
three. Of course, we need self-driving. But make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:35.400
it really hard for this paper - for instance,&nbsp;
train the AI on a large, unannotated self-driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.340
dataset, which means that it does not get a great&nbsp;
deal of help with the training. There is lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:49.080
data, but not a lot of instructions within this&nbsp;
data. Then, finally let’s chuck all these three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:55.980
pieces into a robot, and see if it became smart&nbsp;
enough to be able to get around the real world.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:01.920
Of course, there is no way, right?&nbsp;
That sounds like science fiction and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.560
it might never happen. Well, I hope you know&nbsp;
what’s coming, now hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:15.000
and check out Google’s new self-driving robot,&nbsp;
which they say has all three of these puzzle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
pieces. So, let’s take it out for a ride, and&nbsp;
see what it can do in two amazing experiments.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.240
First, let’s keep things light. Experiment number&nbsp;
one. Let’s ask it to recognize and go to a white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:39.300
building, nice, it found and recognized it. That’s&nbsp;
a good start. Then, continue until you see a white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:49.440
truck, now, continue, until you find a stop&nbsp;
sign. And, we are done! This is incredible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:55.740
we really have all three puzzle pieces working&nbsp;
together. And this is an excellent application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:02.580
of the The Second Law of Papers, which says that&nbsp;
everything is connected. This AI can drive itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:09.720
understands English, and can see an&nbsp;
understand the world around it too. Wow.
 &nbsp;

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:17.280
So now, are you thinking what I am thinking?&nbsp;
Oh yes, let’s give it a really hard time! Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.440
final boss time. Look at this&nbsp;
prompt! Experiment number two.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:30.780
Let’s start following the red-black building until&nbsp;
we see a fire hydrant. And, haha, let’s get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:38.160
bit tricky! Yup, that’s right. No fire hydrant&nbsp;
anywhere to be seen. Your move, little robot!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:47.040
And, look at that! It stops…turns around,&nbsp;
and goes the other way, and finally finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.040
that highly sought after fire hydrant. Very&nbsp;
cool. Now, into the grove to the right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:59.940
Okay, this might be it, and now, you are&nbsp;
supposed to take a right when you see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:06.900
manhole cover. No manhole cover yet, but, there&nbsp;
were ones earlier that it is supposed to ignore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:14.400
so good job not falling for these. And now,&nbsp;
the search continues. But, no manhole cover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:23.700
Still, not one in sight. Wait, got it!&nbsp;
And now, to the right, and find a trailer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:33.540
No trailer anywhere to be seen. Are we even at the&nbsp;
right place? This is getting very confusing. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:39.660
with a little more perseverance, yes, there&nbsp;
is the trailer! Good job little robot!

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:46.440
This is absolutely incredible. I think this&nbsp;
description could have confused many humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.180
yet the robot prevailed. All three&nbsp;
puzzle pieces are working together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:56.280
really well. This looks like something&nbsp;
straight out of a science fiction movie.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:02.580
And a moment that I really loved in this&nbsp;
paper, look. This is a scientist who is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:07.800
trying to keep up with the robot in the&nbsp;
meantime. Imagine this person going home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.360
and being asked - honey, what&nbsp;
have you been doing today? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:18.240
I followed a robot around all day.&nbsp;
It was the best. How cool is that!

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:25.320
So, with DALL-E 2 and Stable Diffusion, we are&nbsp;
definitely entering the age of AI-driven image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:31.260
creation, and with DeepMind’s AlphaFold,&nbsp;
perhaps even the age of AI-driven medicine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:38.400
and we are getting closer and closer to the&nbsp;
age of AI self-driving and navigation too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.640
Loving it. What a time to be alive!

